The Einstein field equations are a highly complex, nonlinear system that can be simplified to a linear system under specific conditions, namely when the energy-momentum tensor is negligible. This linear system is known as the essential collapse solution, which can be used to model the formation of black holes. However, the essential collapse solution is a highly nonlinear system that can be difficult to solve analytically. In this study, we investigate the essential collapse solution using nonlinear statistical frameworks, including the least squares method and the orthogonal collocation method. We compare the accuracy of these frameworks by analyzing their residuals and using the least squares method to estimate the essential collapse solution. Our results show that the orthogonal collocation method provides a more accurate solution than the least squares method, particularly when the number of collocation points is increased. We also demonstrate that the essential collapse solution can be used to model the formation of black holes in a variety of astrophysical scenarios, including the collapse of a star and the merger of two black holes. Our study provides a new approach to solving the essential collapse solution, which can be used to improve our understanding of the formation of black holes and other astrophysical phenomena.\begin{center}
